210 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E (The frame and end shields are manufactured from high-strength cast iron using mature production processes. Aerodynamic optimization improves cooling airflow and heat dissipation.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E (The YBX3 flameproof motor frame uses horizontal, fully distributed cooling fins with a narrowed central section. Fin spacing and concentric-ring arrangements are optimized, with four internal axial ventilation passages. Axially aligned fins at the winding ends increase heat-dissipation area. Corrosion-resistant protective treatments and surface coatings improve durability under climatic exposure. Dual-circuit cooling supports high power density and uniform internal temperature distribution, extending motor service life.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 123 0 R
/T (Optimized Cooling System and Airflow Design)
/E (Optimized Cooling System and Airflow Design)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
213 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E (The YBX3 series employs an optimized cooling-fin arrangement. As motors become more compact and thermal loading increases, improved heat dissipation is essential. The cooling system is designed for effective heat removal during operation.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
214 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E (The motor has two independent airflow paths: an internal circuit and an external circuit. Much of the generated heat is transferred through the stator core to the frame and removed by external cooling air. The remaining heat, primarily from rotor losses and stator winding-end losses, is carried to the frame by internal airflow. External air passes along the cooling fins from the non-drive end toward the drive end. The terminal box is positioned away from the main high-velocity cooling airflow to reduce direct air impact.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
215 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 123 0 R
/Pg 13 0 R
/A [987 0 R 0]
/K [9]
>>
endobj
216 0 obj
<<
